// src/v4/std/algebra.dag
//
// Scope: algebraic primitives that ground every concrete type via inhabitance.
// Anchor: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algebraic_structure (Magma, Monoid, Group, FreeMonoid<T>)
//
// Owns:
//   - the algebra-STRUCTURE hierarchy — the THESIS roots AND the derived
//     structures the substrate's concrete types inhabit:
//       * Magma (closure) → Semigroup → Monoid → Group → AbelianGroup
//       * Semiring → Ring → OrderedRing → Field
//       * ApproximateField — the rounding-aware weakening of Field that
//         std/float.dag inhabits (IEEE-754 rounding breaks
//         associativity/distributivity, so Float is NOT an exact Field;
//         see std/float.dag + DECISIONS.md C5-fidelity)
//       * BooleanAlgebra (classical logic)
//       * FreeMonoid<T> (free constructions)
//     The "roots" (Magma/Monoid/BooleanAlgebra/FreeMonoid) are where the
//     ontology bottoms out; the derived structures are compositions of
//     them — but ALL are declared here, because the scalar files
//     reference them by name (nat→Monoid/Semiring, integer→OrderedRing/
//     AbelianGroup, float→ApproximateField, logic→BooleanAlgebra,
//     text→FreeMonoid). A scalar's grounding is represented, not
//     deferred, iff this file owns the structure it names.
//   - STRUCTURES ONLY. A concrete type declares its OWN inhabitance in
//     its own file (std/integer.dag, std/logic.dag, …); algebra.dag owns
//     what gets inhabited, never the instances (P2).
//
// U1 (operator-ratified 2026-05-15) — the single homomorphism carrier:
//   - ONE carrier: a structure-preserving map between algebras, WITH its
//     realization cost. THESIS:185-186 identities make this one object:
//     coercion = emission; coercion cost = complexity. The back half of
//     the plan is FIVE PHASES over this one carrier, never five engines:
//       Find    = compiler/04_infer.dag (T-9)
//       Realize = compiler/05_emit.dag + 05_eval.dag (T-10/11/22)
//       Measure = lens/cost.dag → lens/complexity.dag (T-12; one
//                 mechanism, two projections — complexity CONSUMES cost,
//                 never re-derives it)
//       Compare = lens/synthesis.dag (T-17)
//   - NO ENGINE discipline (operator 2026-05-15, THESIS no-fallback):
//     every phase is a FAIL-CLOSED lens-read over this carrier. A search
//     that comes up empty (no homomorphism / no inhabitance / no
//     lower-bound model) is a STRUCTURAL ERROR surfaced as a *helpful*
//     Diagnostic (reason + at + Correction) — NEVER fabricated, defaulted,
//     or filled by a fallback. "An engine returns a result when it should
//     return an error" — v4 has lenses, not engines. A standalone cost
//     engine / emit IR / equivalence engine disjoint from this carrier is
//     parallel-representation debt = STOP.
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag: Conj, Disj, Arrow for algebraic structure
//   (the U1 no-engine "empty search → helpful Diagnostic" is surfaced by
//   the consuming lens in its own file — algebra.dag declares structures
//   only and references no Diagnostic)
//
// Status: scaffold — fill per TASKS.md T-2
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).


module v4.std.algebra


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// SCOPE (operator-ratified 2026-05-15): this file owns algebra
// STRUCTURES ONLY. Inhabitance — which concrete type grounds in which
// algebra — is NOT here; the inhabiting type owns its own grounding in
// its own concept file. There is no inhabitance carrier, no instance,
// no concrete-type reference. This file consumes std/node.dag ONLY
// (every `type` below is surface sugar over node.dag's Conj/Disj/Arrow;
// no other module is named).
//
// REFINEMENT IS STRUCTURAL NESTING, not field duplication. A stronger
// algebra CONTAINS the weaker one as a child (Conj composition):
// `Monoid` carries a `semigroup: Semigroup<T>` child plus the
// `identity` it adds — exactly "Monoid<T> is Magma plus an identity
// child" (docs/thesis/epistemic-stacking.md). One authority per
// operation (INVARIANTS P2); changing `op`'s shape touches `Magma`
// alone (cost-of-change = 1). This is the deliberate v4 departure from
// v3's flat re-listing of `op` in every record.
//
// LAWS ARE GROUNDED DEFINITIONS, NOT FIELDS OR FLAGS. Associativity,
// identity, commutativity, distributivity ARE the definitions of the
// named structures (anchor: Wikipedia "Algebraic structure") — math
// consensus, cited per structure below. They are not stored as a flag
// (feedback_no_annotations) and not hand-built equation ASTs here (the
// expression substrate is pipeline-built; a Node data literal would
// need a Symbol the declaration layer cannot mint). The L7 algebra-law
// surface (lens/coverage.dag T-18 + lens/testgen.dag T-19) derives the
// per-axiom TestClaim corpus from each structure's cited definition —
// that derivation is those lenses' concern, not a parallel table here.
//
// NO ENGINE / NO BRIDGE (U1 header discipline). v3's
// `kernel_algebra_profile: Map<String, AlgebraProfile>` and the
// `AlgebraProfile` / `AlgebraFieldTemplate` template tables were a
// string-keyed parallel representation of these structures. They are
// deliberately absent: a name-keyed lookup disjoint from the structure
// it mirrors is exactly the engine debt U1 forbids
// (docs/v4-close-interrogation.md:161 — "not a name-keyed table
// lookup").


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Magma — a carrier with one closed binary operation. The weakest
// structure; the only axiom is closure (the `op` signature returning T
// IS the closure statement — there is nothing to assert separately).
// Anchor: Wikipedia "Magma (algebra)".
type Magma<T> {
  op: fn(T, T) -> T
}

// Semigroup — an associative Magma. Adds the associativity axiom
// (op(op(a,b),c) == op(a,op(b,c))); it adds no operation, so it is
// structurally a Magma. The distinct type carries the proven-associative
// concept (Wikipedia "Semigroup").
type Semigroup<T> {
  magma: Magma<T>
}

// Monoid — a Semigroup with a two-sided identity element.
// Anchor: Wikipedia "Monoid".
type Monoid<T> {
  semigroup: Semigroup<T>
  identity: T
}

// CommutativeMonoid — a Monoid whose op commutes (op(a,b)==op(b,a)).
// Adds only the commutativity axiom (Wikipedia "Monoid#Commutative
// monoid").
type CommutativeMonoid<T> {
  monoid: Monoid<T>
}

// Group — a Monoid in which every element has a two-sided inverse.
// Anchor: Wikipedia "Group (mathematics)".
type Group<T> {
  monoid: Monoid<T>
  inverse: fn(T) -> T
}

// AbelianGroup — a commutative Group. Adds the commutativity axiom
// (Wikipedia "Abelian group").
type AbelianGroup<T> {
  group: Group<T>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Ring-like structures: two interacting operations (additive +
// multiplicative) joined by distributivity + annihilation axioms.
// Modeled as the textbook two-sub-algebra composition (Wikipedia
// "Ring (mathematics)" / "Semiring"): `add` and `mul` are named child
// algebras, so `add.identity` is the zero and `mul.identity` is the
// one — no separate zero/one fields to keep in sync.

// Semiring — additive CommutativeMonoid + multiplicative Monoid, with
// mul distributing over add and the additive identity annihilating
// (distributivity + annihilation are the defining axioms; Wikipedia
// "Semiring").
type Semiring<T> {
  add: CommutativeMonoid<T>
  mul: Monoid<T>
}

// CommutativeSemiring — a Semiring whose multiplication commutes.
type CommutativeSemiring<T> {
  semiring: Semiring<T>
}

// Ring — a Semiring whose additive structure is an AbelianGroup (i.e.
// addition has inverses; `add.group.inverse` is negation).
type Ring<T> {
  add: AbelianGroup<T>
  mul: Monoid<T>
}

// CommutativeRing — a Ring whose multiplication commutes.
type CommutativeRing<T> {
  ring: Ring<T>
}

// OrderedRing — a Ring with a total order compatible with its
// operations. `compare` is the total-order witness; the
// order-compatibility axioms are the definition (Wikipedia "Ordered
// ring").
type OrderedRing<T> {
  ring: Ring<T>
  compare: fn(T, T) -> Ordering
}

// Field — a CommutativeRing in which every nonzero element has a
// multiplicative inverse. That invertibility is the defining AXIOM,
// carried as a grounded definition the same way Semigroup carries
// associativity and CommutativeRing carries commutativity — NOT as a
// stored operation field. There is deliberately no `reciprocal:
// fn(T) -> T`: reciprocal is PARTIAL (undefined at the additive
// identity), so a total `fn(T) -> T` signature would assert a totality
// the operation does not have — the fail-open shape INVARIANTS P3 +
// modeling-discipline.md Practice 2/6 forbid (the illegal state
// "reciprocal(0)" must not be representable at this boundary). The
// reciprocal operation falls out of the axiom; its realization carries
// the nonzero precondition / Tier-2 totalization (Result / Witness /
// refinement-precondition) at the inhabitant per STRUCTURE.md
// architectural commitment #2 — that totalization substrate
// (witness/cardinality) is not consumable from this node.dag-only
// structures file, which is exactly why it is the inhabitant's, not a
// mendacious total field here. Anchor: Wikipedia "Field (mathematics)".
type Field<T> {
  commutative_ring: CommutativeRing<T>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Lattice structures: two idempotent, commutative, associative
// operations linked by absorption. Anchor: Wikipedia "Lattice (order)".

// Lattice — meet and join with the absorption axioms
// (meet(a,join(a,b))==a, join(a,meet(a,b))==a).
type Lattice<T> {
  meet: fn(T, T) -> T
  join: fn(T, T) -> T
}

// BoundedLattice — a Lattice with greatest (top) and least (bottom)
// elements (identities for meet and join respectively).
type BoundedLattice<T> {
  lattice: Lattice<T>
  top: T
  bottom: T
}

// BooleanAlgebra — a complemented distributive BoundedLattice. Adds
// `complement` with the complement axioms (meet(a,complement(a))==
// bottom, join(a,complement(a))==top) plus distributivity. This is the
// algebra classical-logic carriers inhabit (Wikipedia "Boolean
// algebra (structure)").
type BooleanAlgebra<T> {
  bounded_lattice: BoundedLattice<T>
  complement: fn(T) -> T
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FreeMonoid<T> — the free monoid over T: finite sequences of T under
// concatenation, with no relations beyond the monoid axioms. Unlike the
// structures above (operation-records imposed on an existing carrier),
// a free construction IS a canonical carrier — THESIS:194 names it a
// root primitive precisely for this reason, so it is declared directly
// as its recursive shape rather than derived from Monoid. Concrete
// sequence types ground by aliasing onto it (e.g. a text type onto
// FreeMonoid over a char type) — that aliasing is the inhabitant's
// concern, not this file's. Anchor: Wikipedia "Free monoid".
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). The two
// variants are the constructors of an inductive type (base + cons),
// not compressed structure. Ledger — four patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: Empty/Cons are not per-consumer facts;
//      every structural fold over a FreeMonoid handles BOTH (induction
//      base + step). No consumer uses only one.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the variants have DIFFERENT shapes
//      (Empty nullary; Cons {head, tail}). Collapsing to {kind, head?,
//      tail?} makes "Cons with no head" representable — the inverse of
//      P2 (illegal states unrepresentable).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: this declaration IS the std/ algebraic
//      source other types ground in (THESIS:194 root primitive;
//      epistemic-stacking.md). There is no richer source to point at —
//      pointing it elsewhere is circular.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a sequence is EITHER empty OR head+tail;
//      genuine alternatives (recursion base vs inductive case), never
//      simultaneous coordinates. No value is "both".
// Terminal: irreducible inductive definition of the free monoid; a
// root primitive grounded in monoid theory.
type FreeMonoid<T>
  = Empty
  | Cons { head: T, tail: FreeMonoid<T> }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Ordering — the result of a total-order comparison. Consumed by
// OrderedRing.compare. Anchor: order theory (trichotomy: exactly one
// of <, =, > holds).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// four patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: a comparison result is consumed
//      uniformly (every `compare` consumer branches on all three); not
//      a scattered per-consumer fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: promoting to `{ sign: Int }` (-1/0/+1)
//      replaces a closed 3-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded Int
//      (sign=42 representable) — violates P2 + M4 (closed sets are
//      enums, not numeric proxies). The enum IS the faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: Less/Equal/Greater do not trace to
//      distinct std/ structures; they ARE the codomain of the order
//      relation (trichotomy). No richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a comparison yields EXACTLY ONE of three
//      (total-order trichotomy: exactly one of a<b, a=b, a>b holds) —
//      mutually-exclusive alternatives, not simultaneous coordinates.
// Terminal: irreducible trichotomy codomain of a total order.
type Ordering
  = Less
  | Equal
  | Greater


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Homomorphism<Source, Target> — THE single carrier (U1, header).
//
// A structure-preserving map between two algebras OF THE SAME CLASS. A
// homomorphism preserves exactly one algebra structure, so the carrier
// is a closed coproduct with one variant per class; each variant pairs
// the SAME class on both sides (`Magma<Source>` with `Magma<Target>`,
// etc.). Cross-class pairs (e.g. a Ring source with a Lattice target)
// are therefore STRUCTURALLY UNREPRESENTABLE — not pushed to a later
// validator (INVARIANTS P2 + modeling-discipline.md Practice 2/6:
// illegal states unrepresentable at the carrier boundary). This is the
// only v2-expressible shape that enforces same-class: a single
// shared-class discriminant would need dependent typing, and per-class
// homomorphism *types* would violate U1's ONE-carrier mandate — so it
// is ONE coproduct type, the single carrier, variants discriminating
// the preserved class.
//
// Each variant carries both same-class structures (the preserved-
// algebra fact flows forward intact — P2 facts-flow-forward, the
// witness.dag "Holds CARRIES the proven fact" discipline) plus `map`,
// the carrier-level function. "Structure-preserving" is the LAW each
// variant asserts (every operation/constant preserved), verified by
// the L7 algebra-law surface against the variant's source/target,
// never a flag (feedback_no_annotations). Find (compiler/04_infer.dag
// T-9) binds the variant; Realize/Measure/L7 read it.
//
// FreeMonoid is deliberately ABSENT as a variant. A free monoid is a
// monoid; its structure-preserving maps ARE monoid homomorphisms
// (`HomMonoid` on the free monoid's carrier). Giving it its own
// variant would force `Algebra<T>`-style parameter conflation —
// `FreeMonoid<T>`'s `T` is the GENERATOR while every other class's
// parameter is the CARRIER (openai-pro 12674 / THESIS:194-195
// "String inhabits FreeMonoid<Char>"). Binding generator↔carrier is an
// inhabitance concern, explicitly struck from T-2 (operator-ratified);
// routing free-monoid maps through `HomMonoid` keeps that out of this
// structures-only file and stays faithful (category Mon).
//
// "WITH its realization cost" (U1): this is the ONE carrier all five
// phases read — Find binds it, Realize projects it, Measure folds its
// cost FROM `map`'s own structure, Compare (lens/synthesis) reads that.
// There is deliberately NO cost field per variant: a stored cost would
// be a representation parallel to the map it measures — the engine debt
// U1 forbids (THESIS:185-186 coercion = emission, coercion cost =
// complexity: one object, many lens projections).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every consumer (Find/Realize/Measure/L7)
//      reads the same carrier to validate preservation; not a
//      per-consumer scatter.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the per-class source/target structures
//      have genuinely different shapes (Magma vs Ring vs Lattice); no
//      uniform label space to fold to one record. Collapsing also
//      re-admits the cross-class illegal state this shape exists to
//      forbid.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: variants ARE references
//      to the std/ algebra structures (pattern 3's own target shape);
//      the closed per-class selector is structurally required because
//      same-class enforcement has no v2 dependent-typing alternative.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a homomorphism preserves EXACTLY ONE class;
//      genuine alternatives, never coordinates.
// Terminal: the closed taxonomy of homomorphism classes over this
// file's structure-imposed algebras (anchor: Wikipedia "Homomorphism";
// a fixed finite set). No richer source; the taxonomy IS the source.
// Cost-of-change: a new structure-imposed algebra adds its declaration
// + one variant here.
type Homomorphism<Source, Target>
  = HomMagma { source: Magma<Source>, target: Magma<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomSemigroup { source: Semigroup<Source>, target: Semigroup<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomMonoid { source: Monoid<Source>, target: Monoid<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomCommutativeMonoid { source: CommutativeMonoid<Source>, target: CommutativeMonoid<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomGroup { source: Group<Source>, target: Group<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomAbelianGroup { source: AbelianGroup<Source>, target: AbelianGroup<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomSemiring { source: Semiring<Source>, target: Semiring<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomCommutativeSemiring { source: CommutativeSemiring<Source>, target: CommutativeSemiring<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomRing { source: Ring<Source>, target: Ring<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomCommutativeRing { source: CommutativeRing<Source>, target: CommutativeRing<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomOrderedRing { source: OrderedRing<Source>, target: OrderedRing<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomField { source: Field<Source>, target: Field<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomLattice { source: Lattice<Source>, target: Lattice<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomBoundedLattice { source: BoundedLattice<Source>, target: BoundedLattice<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }
  | HomBooleanAlgebra { source: BooleanAlgebra<Source>, target: BooleanAlgebra<Target>, map: fn(Source) -> Target }

